            %p
              In this form the
              %em classical Master Ward identity
              was originally identified in (
              %a{:href => "master+Ward+identity#DuetschFredenhagen02"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen 02, (90)
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "master+Ward+identity#BrennecketDuetsch07"} Brennecke-Dütsch 07, (5.5)
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "master+Ward+identity#DuetschBoas02"} Dütsch-Boas 02
          %h3#RenormalizationAndMasterWardIdentity Renormalization and Master ward identity
          %p
            The quantum master equation in the form of prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#QuantumMasterEquation"}
            is derived on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi int
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{int}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            terms of interest are point-interactions, hence are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+observables"} local observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension"} extension
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            and hence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+S-matrix"} perturbative S-matrix
            from regular to local onservables exsists but involves choices, these are the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            choices in the formulation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
          %p
            Since for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauged fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            this physically relevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            are not the plain (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"}> mcirocausal
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+observables"} polynomial observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+differential"} BV-BRST differential
            on them, one needs to require for gauge theories that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            still holds after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization+condition"} renormalization condition
            called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/master+Ward+identity"} master Ward identity
            (
            %a{:href => "#Rejzner11"}> Rejzner 11 (prop. 5.3.1) and following paragraphs
            ). If the quantum master equation cannot be retained in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            one says that the field theory suffers from a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/master+Ward+identity"} master Ward identity
          %h2#references References
          %p The concept originates with
          %ul
            %li#BatalinVilkovisky81
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Batalin"}> Igor Batalin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigori+Vilkovisky"} Grigori Vilkovisky
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gauge Algebra and Quantization
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(81)90205-7"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(81)90205-7
          %p Traditional review includes
          %ul
            %li#HenneauxTeitelboim92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Teitelboim"} Claudio Teitelboim
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantization+of+Gauge+Systems"} Quantization of Gauge Systems
          %p
            Discussion in the rigorous context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            formulated in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            is in:
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in the functional approach to classical field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5112"} arXiv:1101.5112
            %li#FredenhagenRejzner11b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"} Kasia Rejzner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"} arXiv:1110.5232
            %li#Rejzner11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in locally covariant field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5130"}> arXiv:1111.5130
                )
            %li#Rejzner13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
                ,
                %em Remarks on local symmetry invariance in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.7037"}> arXiv:1301.7037
                )
          %p and surveyed in
          %ul
            %li#Rejzner16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kasia+Rejzner"}> Kasia Rejzner
              , section 7 of
              %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
              Springer 2016 (
              %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-319-25901-7"}> web
              )
